---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base title="The Door Mouse Who Saved the World | Word And Mouth" description="How eccentric academics, a shy publisher, and a bathtub genius changed the course of WWI — by fighting their own bureaucracy.">
  <article class="case-study">
    <section class="case-header">
      <a href="/solved/" class="back-link">← Back to Solved</a>
      <div class="case-meta">
        <span class="problem-tag">Visibility Problem</span>
        <span class="case-date">WWI / January 1917</span>
        <span class="case-time">8 min read</span>
      </div>
      <h1>The Door Mouse Who Saved the World</h1>
      <p class="case-subtitle">Room 40 was a British intelligence unit that cracked the code that brought America into WWI. Their biggest enemy wasn't Germany — it was their own organisation.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="case-format">
      <div class="format-card problem-everyone-saw">
        <span class="format-number">1</span>
        <h2>The Problem Everyone Saw</h2>
        <p>"We need to crack German codes to win the war."</p>
        <p>In 1914, Britain cut Germany's transatlantic cables — the first act of cyberwarfare. Germany was forced onto Swedish and American cable routes, which Britain could intercept. The obvious problem: decrypt the messages.</p>
        <p>So they assembled the best minds they could find. Not spies. Academics. Failed diplomats. Hobbyists. Men who did their best thinking in bathtubs.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="format-card problem-actually-there">
        <span class="format-number">2</span>
        <h2>The Problem That Was Actually There</h2>
        <p><strong>Proximity without communication.</strong> The codebreakers could see the bombs in the messages. They just weren't allowed to say so.</p>
        
        <div class="insight-box">
          <h3>The Bureaucratic Wall</h3>
          <p>Room 40's brilliant analysts were <em>"not permitted to understand or interpret the information themselves."</em> They had to pass "raw data" to "Naval specialists" who lacked the context to understand it.</p>
          <p>At the Battle of Jutland, this wall cost lives. The codebreakers knew where the German fleet was. The admirals didn't ask. Nobody connected the dots.</p>
          <blockquote>"The secrecy of Room 40 was not physical but bureaucratic."</blockquote>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="format-card unexpected-solution">
        <span class="format-number">3</span>
        <h2>The Unexpected Solution</h2>
        <p><strong>Be the "door mouse" who runs.</strong></p>
        
        <div class="story-highlight">
          <p>On January 17, 1917, Nigel de Grey and William Montgomery decoded a secret German message: Mexico was being offered an alliance against America. If they could prove it, America would enter the war.</p>
          <p>De Grey was shy, physically small, and had failed his diplomatic exams because his Italian wasn't good enough. He was a publisher who just happened to be good at puzzles.</p>
          <p>What did he do?</p>
          <p><strong>He ran.</strong></p>
          <p>He sprinted down narrow corridors, burst into his boss's office, and shouted:</p>
          <blockquote>"Do you want America in the war, Sir?"</blockquote>
        </div>
        
        <p>The rest required improvisation, bribes, and fishing — literally fishing codebooks from the sea. But the breakthrough came from a "schlub" recognising the moment and running.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="format-card what-you-can-steal">
        <span class="format-number">4</span>
        <h2>What You Can Steal</h2>
        
        <div class="steal-list">
          <div class="steal-item">
            <h4>1. Proximity Without Communication Is Worse Than Distance</h4>
            <p>Room 40 was 40 feet from the admirals who needed their intelligence. But the wall between "analysts" and "decision-makers" cost more than distance would have. Check your handoff points.</p>
          </div>
          
          <div class="steal-item">
            <h4>2. Imperfection Is a Feature</h4>
            <p>Every hero in this story succeeded <em>because</em> of their quirks. Dilly Knox did his best work in a bathtub. Blinker Hall used his facial tic to intimidate enemies. Don't smooth out the edges that make you effective.</p>
          </div>
          
          <div class="steal-item">
            <h4>3. Sometimes You Just Have to Run</h4>
            <p>De Grey didn't have permission. He didn't schedule a meeting. He ran. The lesson: recognise the moment — and don't wait for bureaucracy to catch up.</p>
          </div>
          
          <div class="steal-item">
            <h4>4. Build the Index Before You Need It</h4>
            <p>The "Ladies of the Index" maintained 100,000+ cross-referenced code groups. They weren't analysts — officially. But their "human Google" made every breakthrough possible. Value the infrastructure that enables genius.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="case-verdict">
      <h2>The Twist</h2>
      <p>Room 40's success created their next problem. The intelligence they generated was so valuable that it was eventually leaked — and Germany, horrified, embarked on intensive programmes to improve their cryptography.</p>
      <p>The direct result: <strong>the Enigma machine.</strong></p>
      <p>The heroes of 1917 literally created the monster they'd have to fight in 1939. Success bred the conditions for the next failure — and the next generation of door mice.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="case-cta">
      <h3>Is your organisation's biggest enemy... your organisation?</h3>
      <p>We find the walls between data and decision. Then we tear them down.</p>
      <a href="mailto:dave@wordandmouth.com?subject=Organisational Walls Audit" class="cta-button">Start the Conversation</a>
    </section>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .case-study {
    max-width: 800px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 0 2rem;
  }

  .case-header {
    padding: 10rem 0 3rem;
  }

  .back-link {
    display: inline-block;
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    margin-bottom: 2rem;
    transition: color 0.2s;
  }

  .back-link:hover {
    color: var(--accent);
  }

  .case-meta {
    display: flex;
    gap: 1rem;
    align-items: center;
    margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    color: var(--muted);
    flex-wrap: wrap;
  }

  .problem-tag {
    background: rgba(255, 107, 53, 0.15);
    color: var(--accent);
    padding: 0.25rem 0.6rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    font-size: 0.7rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.05em;
  }

  .case-header h1 {
    font-size: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 2.75rem);
    line-height: 1.15;
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }

  .case-subtitle {
    font-size: 1.2rem;
    color: var(--muted);
    line-height: 1.5;
  }

  .case-format {
    padding: 2rem 0 3rem;
  }

  .format-card {
    background: #111;
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    padding: 2rem;
    margin-bottom: 2rem;
    position: relative;
  }

  .format-number {
    position: absolute;
    top: -12px;
    left: 20px;
    background: var(--accent);
    color: #000;
    width: 28px;
    height: 28px;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }

  .format-card.problem-everyone-saw { border-left: 3px solid #666; }
  .format-card.problem-actually-there { border-left: 3px solid var(--accent); }
  .format-card.unexpected-solution { border-left: 3px solid #4CAF50; }
  .format-card.what-you-can-steal { border-left: 3px solid #2196F3; }

  .format-card h2 {
    font-size: 1.3rem;
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }

  .format-card p {
    font-size: 1rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: #ccc;
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }

  .insight-box, .story-highlight {
    background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3);
    padding: 1.5rem;
    margin-top: 1rem;
  }

  .insight-box h3 {
    font-size: 1rem;
    color: var(--accent);
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }

  blockquote {
    border-left: 3px solid var(--accent);
    padding-left: 1rem;
    font-style: italic;
    color: #aaa;
    margin: 1rem 0;
  }

  .steal-list {
    display: grid;
    gap: 1.5rem;
  }

  .steal-item h4 {
    font-size: 1rem;
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
    color: #2196F3;
  }

  .steal-item p {
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    color: var(--muted);
    margin: 0;
  }

  .case-verdict {
    padding: 2rem 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
  }

  .case-verdict h2 {
    font-size: 1.5rem;
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }

  .case-verdict p {
    font-size: 1.1rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }

  .case-cta {
    background: linear-gradient(135deg, #1a1a1a 0%, #0f0f0f 100%);
    padding: 2.5rem;
    text-align: center;
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    margin: 2rem 0;
  }

  .case-cta h3 { font-size: 1.3rem; margin-bottom: 0.75rem; }
  .case-cta p { color: var(--muted); margin-bottom: 1.5rem; }

  .cta-button {
    display: inline-block;
    background: var(--accent);
    color: #000;
    padding: 0.75rem 1.5rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  @media (max-width: 768px) {
    .case-study { padding: 0 1.25rem; }
    .case-header { padding: 8rem 0 2rem; }
    .format-card { padding: 1.5rem; }
  }
</style>
</article>
